{
  "_about": {
    "purpose": "Hand-labelled symbol ground truth for the expanded per-language parsing corpus.",
    "labelling_rules": [
      "One entry per definition the source file actually writes, as [kind, name].",
      "Entries are a multiset: two methods named 'area' in two classes are two entries.",
      "A declaration without a body (an interface or protocol member) is a definition of that member and is labelled.",
      "A constructor is labelled as a method under the name the language writes it with.",
      "In a file whose name marks it as a test file, every function-like symbol is labelled 'test'. A class in a test file keeps kind 'class': it is the container, not the test. This is the project's stated convention and the labels follow it.",
      "A struct, record, or enum is labelled by what its own grammar makes it, not by a rule imposed across languages. Where a grammar folds struct, class, and enum into one node type it is labelled 'class' (Swift struct Point, C++ struct Point, Java enum Kind); where a grammar gives it a distinct node type it is labelled 'type' (C struct, Zig struct, VB.NET Structure, Go type). This is deliberately not uniform, because the parse is not uniform, and pretending otherwise would mean labelling something the parser cannot know.",
      "Module symbols are excluded from scoring: every file has exactly one and scoring it would inflate every language equally."
    ],
    "label_corrections": [
      "typescript/shapes.ts: the interface member `draw(): void` was omitted, while the Java, C#, Kotlin, Swift, Solidity, and Rust equivalents in this same corpus were all labelled. An independent review found the inconsistency; the label was added and the parser now walks interface bodies.",
      "rust/shapes.rs: the trait method declaration `fn draw(&self);` was omitted from the labels. The rules above say a declaration without a body is a definition of that member, and the Java and C# interface members in this same corpus are labelled, so the omission was a labelling error and was corrected."
    ],
    "fidelity_values": [
      "grammar: a real Tree-sitter parse of the whole file.",
      "composite: the file is unwrapped (a notebook's code cells, a component's script block, an infrastructure file's block structure) and then parsed with another language's grammar. The parse itself is real; the unwrapping is this project's own code.",
      "fallback: the documented pattern extractor, used where no permissive grammar is installable. Never presented as a parse."
    ],
    "known_metric_limits": [
      "The (kind, name) pair cannot distinguish two definitions that share both, so a companion object named after its class collapses into one entry. The corpus avoids that case rather than hiding it."
    ]
  },
